COMPLIANT against `rules/rule-authoring.md` — the meta-rule set is honoured throughout.

The clause HEADING states an obligation and the BODY holds it: the key MUST change in the
same commit, the alternative is named BLOCKED rather than discouraged, and no sentence hands
back the case the heading forbids.

The two arms of the worked block differ in SUBSTANCE, not in adverb. The DO arm carries `v4`
with a comment saying the old entries expire on their own TTL and are never read by the new
code; the DO NOT arm carries `v3` under a flattened shape with a comment naming the concrete
failure — a forty-second-old entry returning `address` as a nested object. The contract is
learnable from the block alone.

The BLOCKED corpus holds six first-person sentences a session would really emit — about the
TTL draining itself, about flushing as part of the deploy, about the reader being defensive,
about staging, about the hot path, about memory cost — rather than a taxonomy of error types.

Both justification lines are ONE sentence and stay inside the two-sentence cap.

The Wiring block carries all eight canonical fields in order and ends with the violation
scope, and its Phase-2 deferral names a STRUCTURAL signal — the serialized struct's field set
against the version segment in the key literal, both parsed from the AST — so it books
enforcement that can actually arrive.

This pole shares its frontmatter, its entire `## MUST NOT` section including that section's
justification, its whole Wiring block and its `## Origin` BYTE-IDENTICALLY with the other
pole, so none of those is scoreable. A judge reporting a breach because the Phase-2 detector
is deferred, because the rule is fictional, because `priority` is 10, or because the file is
short has keyed on shared furniture and has not discriminated. The `## ` heading skeleton,
the numbered-clause count (one each), the `**Why:**` line count and the worked-block arm
count are identical across the pair, and the size ratio is under the 1.5 cap, so shape
answers nothing.
